DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     ddbb examines and patches object files, core dumps, system
     dumps and plain files, and runs programs under ppttrraaccee
     control with breakpoints and single stepping.  It works at
     the machine level: it disassembles Z8001 instructions and
     displays memory in the formats described below, but it reads
     the symbol table of the object file so that addresses can be
     given and printed by name.  Commands are read from the stan-
     dard input.

     ddbb sees a program as three _a_d_d_r_e_s_s _s_p_a_c_e_s: the data space
     (dd), the instruction space (ii) and the user area of the
     process (uu), which holds the saved registers.  Each space is
     described by a list of address maps (see :mm) that tell where
     a range of addresses is to be found: in a file, or in a run-
     ning child process through ppttrraaccee. What is mapped depends on
     the mode selected by the options.

     -oo   _d_a_t_a_f_i_l_e is an object file (the default when no option
          is given and one file is named).  With two files, _m_a_p-
          _f_i_l_e supplies the symbol table and _d_a_t_a_f_i_l_e is the ob-
          ject that is examined and patched.  With no file, ll.oouutt
          is used.

     -cc   _d_a_t_a_f_i_l_e is a core file written by a user process.  Its
          user area and every dumped segment are mapped; pure
          segments and the symbol table come from _m_a_p_f_i_l_e, which
          must be the object the process was running (ddbb
          complains "Core different from object" otherwise).
          With no file, ll.oouutt and ccoorree are used.  This is also
          the default when two files are named without an option.

     -dd   _d_a_t_a_f_i_l_e is a system dump; _m_a_p_f_i_l_e is the kernel.  With
          no file, /ccoohheerreenntt and /ddeevv/dduummpp are used.

     -kk   Map _d_a_t_a_f_i_l_e as a kernel image in memory: the whole
          file is the instruction space and the user area, and
          the data space starts at the kernel's eetteexxtt_ symbol.
          With no file, /ccoohheerreenntt and /ddeevv/mmeemm are used, so a
          running system can be examined.

     -ff   Map _d_a_t_a_f_i_l_e as a plain array of bytes, in both the
          data and the instruction space.  A _m_a_p_f_i_l_e may supply a
          symbol table.

     -ee   The next argument is an object file to be executed as a
          traced child process; the remaining arguments are
          passed to it as its command line.  This lets the shell


