EExxpprreessssiioonnss aanndd AAddddrreesssseess
     Most commands take an address and a count, both written as
     expressions.  An expression is built from

     _n_u_m_b_e_r
           An integer constant, hexadecimal by default.  A
           leading 00 makes it octal, a leading 00xx hexadecimal and
           a leading # decimal; the choice stays in effect for
           later constants.

     .     The current address, which every display command
           leaves after the last item shown.

     _s_y_m_b_o_l
           A name from the symbol table.  The name may be typed
           as the C compiler wrote it (mmaaiinn_) or in C form
           (mmaaiinn); the first eight characters are significant.
           The kind of a symbol decides the space of the expres-
           sion: text symbols name the instruction space, data
           and bss symbols the data space.

     dd, ii, uu
           Address 0 of the data space, the instruction space and
           the user area.  Adding to one of these selects that
           space explicitly.

     ppcc, rr_n, rrrr_n, rrhh_n, rrll_n
           The registers of the traced process, as addresses in
           the user area, so they can be displayed and patched
           like any other location.

     The binary operators are +, -, * and / with the usual
     precedence, the unary operators are - (negation), ~
     (complement) and * (the word at the address), and paren-
     theses group.  All arithmetic is done in longs.  An expres-
     sion takes its space from its leftmost operand.

     Where a command accepts several expressions they are
     separated by commas.  An omitted address defaults to ., an
     omitted count to 1.


