     [_a_d_d_r_e_s_s][,_c_o_u_n_t]?[_f_o_r_m_a_t]
           Display _c_o_u_n_t items starting at _a_d_d_r_e_s_s, each accor-
           ding to _f_o_r_m_a_t. The format string is remembered per
           space and is reused when omitted; initially it is ww
           for the data space and the user area and ii for the in-
           struction space.  Every item starts a line with its
           address and moves .  past what it displayed.  The for-
           mat is a sequence of the letters below, each option-
           ally preceded by a repeat count:

           bb   A byte.

           ww   A word (16 bits); hh is a synonym.

           ll   A long (32 bits).

           vv   A three-byte value, as stored in a file system.

           cc   A character, with control characters and the back-
               slash shown as C escapes.

           CC   A character, printed as is.

           ss   A string ending in a null byte, escaped as for cc.

           SS   The same string without escapes.

           ff   A single-precision floating point number.

           FF   A double-precision floating point number.

           ii   A machine instruction, disassembled.

           pp   A long taken as an address, printed symbolically.

           YY   A long taken as a time, printed as by ddaattee.

           dd, oo, uu, xx
               Select decimal, octal, unsigned or hexadecimal for
               the numeric formats that follow; hexadecimal is
               the default.

           +, -
               Move the address forward or back by the size of
               the last item without displaying anything.

           ^   Return to the address the command started at.

           nn   Start a new output line.

