     A program is started under trace control with :ee (or the -ee
     option), after which it can be run to breakpoints and single
     stepped.  The child stops at every breakpoint and every
     fault; the fault or the breakpoint's commands are then run
     and ddbb reads commands again.

     :ee [_a_r_g_u_m_e_n_t ...]
           Start the object file as a child process with the
           given arguments (up to 19).  Quotes and backslashes
           are honoured; <, > and >> redirect the child's stan-
           dard input and output.  A child already running is
           killed first.

     [_a_d_d_r_e_s_s]:bb[_c_o_m_m_a_n_d_s]
           Set a breakpoint at _a_d_d_r_e_s_s and run _c_o_m_m_a_n_d_s whenever
           it is hit.  The default command string displays the
           address symbolically and disassembles the instruction
           there, then waits for commands.  Up to 15 breakpoints
           may be set.

     [_a_d_d_r_e_s_s]:bbrr[_c_o_m_m_a_n_d_s]
           Set a return breakpoint: it is taken when the function
           containing the current pc returns to _a_d_d_r_e_s_s.

     [_a_d_d_r_e_s_s]:dd
           Delete the breakpoint at _a_d_d_r_e_s_s; :ddrr deletes a return
           breakpoint and :ddss a step-over breakpoint, and :ddaa
           deletes them all.

     :pp    List the breakpoints.

     [_a_d_d_r_e_s_s]:cc
           Continue the child, at _a_d_d_r_e_s_s if one is given.


