     [_a_d_d_r_e_s_s][,_c_o_u_n_t]:ss[cc][_c_o_m_m_a_n_d_s]
           Single step _c_o_u_n_t instructions, starting at _a_d_d_r_e_s_s if
           given, and run _c_o_m_m_a_n_d_s after each; by default the in-
           struction at the new pc is disassembled.  With :sscc, a
           ccaallll instruction is stepped over: the called function
           runs at full speed and stepping resumes on return.
           While stepping, an empty command line steps once more.

     :rr    Display the registers of the stopped child.

     [_d_e_p_t_h]:tt
           Print a traceback of the child's stack, one line per
           active function with the arguments it was called with,
           to _d_e_p_t_h levels (default: all).

     :ff    Print the fault that stopped the child, or that
           produced the core file.

     :mm    Print the address maps of the three spaces.

     [_e_x_p_r]:xx
           When _e_x_p_r is nonzero (the default), read commands from
           the standard input until end of file or a :qq with a
           zero argument.  Breakpoint command strings use this to
           stop and wait for the user.

     [_e_x_p_r]:qq
           When _e_x_p_r is nonzero (the default), kill the child if
           any and exit.  End of file on the standard input has
           the same effect.

     An interrupt from the terminal stops a running display and
     is reported as "Interrupt"; it does not kill the child.

